# .dockerignore
#
# What never enters the build context.
#
# A smaller context builds faster, but the reason this file matters more than that is
# REQ-N-002: a populated local .env is excluded here as well as in .gitignore, so it
# cannot be baked into an image layer even by accident. A secret in a layer survives
# deletion of the file, which is exactly the failure mode that requirement exists to
# prevent.
#
# This duplicates part of .gitignore, and a second list can drift from the first. The
# alternative is not available - the Docker daemon does not read .gitignore.

# Version control, CI configuration and governance documents. None are needed at
# runtime; the image serves the application, not the repository.
.git/
.gitattributes
.gitignore
.github/
docs/

# D-011: the local virtual environment. The image installs its own dependencies from
# requirements.txt, and a Windows .venv is unusable on Linux in any case.
.venv/

# REQ-N-002. .env.example holds no values, but it is not needed at runtime either.
.env
.env.*

# Claude working artefacts. Never a deliverable, never needed in an image.
dev_reports/

# Local database. The container's database lives on a mounted volume (D-002), never
# in a layer, so copying one in would only shadow the real thing confusingly.
*.sqlite3
db.sqlite3

# Tests and quality caches. The suite runs in CI and locally, not in the runtime
# image.
tests/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
coverage.xml
htmlcov/

# Static files collected by a local run. The container collects its own at start-up.
staticfiles/

# Python bytecode
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# OS and editor
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.idea/
